<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-direct-variation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Direct Variation</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.0 Numbers and Algebra
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.5 Formulae and Variations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Solve direct and inverse variations from real life situations</li>
                        <li>Draw graphs of direct and inverse variations</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
    </objectives>

   <axiom component="lesson-plan">
    <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
    <dl>
        <li>
            <title>Suggested Time</title>
            <p>
                15 minutes
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>Objective</title>
            <p>
                To enable learners to discover the concept of direct variation by investigating the relationship between fuel consumed and distance travelled, identifying the constant ratio between the two quantities, and interpreting its meaning in a real-world context.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>Teacher's Role</title>
            <p>
                Organize learners into groups of 3–4, facilitate completion of the fuel-efficiency table, and guide discussions about the calculated ratios. Encourage learners to identify patterns in the relationship between fuel consumed and distance travelled without directly introducing the formal rule. Prompt learners to explain why the ratio remains constant and what it represents in terms of vehicle efficiency.
            </p>
        </li>
    </dl>
</axiom>

<activity>
    <title>The Fuel Efficiency Investigation</title>
    <statement>
        <p><em>In groups of 3–4 students</em></p>
        
        <p><strong>Part 1: Data Collection &amp; Ratios</strong></p>
        <p>
            The distance (<m>d</m>) a vehicle travels is directly proportional to the amount of fuel (<m>f</m>) it consumes. Imagine tracking five different vehicles traveling along the highway, each consuming fuel at a rate of 20 km per liter.
        </p>
        
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Fill in the missing total distances for each vehicle in the table below, then calculate the ratio of <m>\frac{\text{Distance}(d)}{\text{Fuel}(f)}</m> for each row.
                </p>
                <table>
                    <title>Vehicle Fuel Consumption and Ratios</title>
                    <tabular halign="left">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                            <cell>Vehicle</cell>
                            <cell>Fuel Consumed (<m>f</m>) in Liters</cell>
                            <cell>Total Distance (<m>d</m>) in km</cell>
                            <cell>Ratio (<m>\frac{d}{f}</m>)</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Motorbike</cell>
                            <cell>2</cell>
                            <cell>40</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Probox</cell>
                            <cell>5</cell>
                            <cell>100</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Matatu</cell>
                            <cell>10</cell>
                            <cell>200</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Bus</cell>
                            <cell>15</cell>
                            <cell>300</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Lorry</cell>
                            <cell>20</cell>
                            <cell>400</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    What do you notice about the calculated ratio <m>\frac{d}{f}</m> across all vehicles? What practical real-world performance metric does this constant number represent?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>

       <p><strong>Part 2: Plotting and Investigating the Graph</strong></p>
        <ol start="3">
            <li>
                <p>
                    On a cartesian grid, let Fuel Consumed be the horizontal axis (<m>f</m>) and Distance be the vertical axis (<m>d</m>). Plot the points for all five vehicles and draw a straight line through them, starting from the origin <m>(0,0)</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The steepness (gradient) of your line represents your constant of proportionality, <m>k</m> (the fuel efficiency of 20 km/l).
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Discuss how the line would change if you plotted:
                    <ul>
                        <li>A highly efficient hybrid car at 30 km/l (<m>k = 30</m>).</li>
                        <li>A heavy lorry at only 8 km/l (<m>k = 8</m>).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Fill in the blanks based on your observations:
                    <ul>
                        <li>A larger value of <m>k</m> (better efficiency) makes the line <fillin characters="10"/> (steeper / flatter).</li>
                        <li>A smaller value of <m>k</m> (poorer efficiency) makes the line <fillin characters="10"/> (steeper / flatter).</li>
                        <li>Every direct variation graph is a straight line passing through the <fillin characters="10"/> (origin / y-axis).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </statement>
</activity>

   <axiom component="lesson-plan">
    <title>Structured Instruction</title>
    <p>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalize learners' discoveries by introducing the definition, equation, graphical representation, and interpretation of direct variation using fuel-efficiency data.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> Refer learners to the ratios they calculated in the Fuel Efficiency Investigation. Highlight that although the amounts of fuel and distances travelled were different, the ratio <m>\frac{d}{f}</m> remained constant at 20. Introduce this constant value as the constant of proportionality, <m>k</m>, leading to the equation <m>d = kf</m>.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Emphasize that when the amount of fuel consumed doubles, triples, or is multiplied by any factor, the distance travelled changes by the same factor, provided the fuel efficiency remains constant.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Demonstrate that the graph of a direct variation is a straight line passing through the origin and explain that its gradient represents the constant of proportionality, <m>k</m>, which in this investigation is the fuel efficiency measured in kilometres per litre.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Connect the concept to other real-life situations involving direct variation such as wages earned per hour worked, circumference and diameter of a circle, quantities purchased at a fixed price, and distance travelled at a constant speed.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </paragraphs>
</axiom>

    <insight>
    <title>Key Takeaway: Direct Variation</title>

    <p>
        Direct variation occurs when two quantities change in the same proportion. As one quantity increases or decreases, the other changes by the same factor.
    </p>

    <p>
        In the Fuel Efficiency Investigation, the ratio of distance travelled to fuel consumed remained constant:
    </p>

    <p>
        <m>\frac{d}{f}=20</m>
    </p>

    <p>
        This constant value represents the fuel efficiency of the vehicle and is called the <em>constant of proportionality</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
        The relationship can therefore be written as:
    </p>

    <p>
        <m>d = kf</m>
    </p>

    <p>
        where:
    </p>

    <list>
        <item><m>d</m> represents the distance travelled.</item>
        <item><m>f</m> represents the fuel consumed.</item>
        <item><m>k</m> represents the constant of proportionality (fuel efficiency in km/l).</item>
    </list>

    <p>
        A direct variation graph is always a straight line passing through the origin because when no fuel is consumed, no distance can be travelled.
    </p>

    <list>
        <item>The gradient of the graph equals the constant of proportionality, <m>k</m>.</item>
        <item>A larger value of <m>k</m> produces a steeper graph because more distance is travelled for each litre of fuel.</item>
        <item>A smaller value of <m>k</m> produces a flatter graph because less distance is travelled for each litre of fuel.</item>
    </list>

    <table>
        <title>Direct Variation Features</title>
        <tabular halign="left">
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                <cell>Feature</cell>
                <cell>Direct Variation Rule</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>Equation</cell>
                <cell><m>y = kx</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>Graph Shape</cell>
                <cell>Straight line</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>Origin Point</cell>
                <cell>Passes through <m>(0,0)</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>Constant</cell>
                <cell><m>\frac{y}{x}=k</m></cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
    </table>
</insight>


     <p>
        Direct variation describes relationships where the ratio between two quantities remains constant, allowing us to predict one quantity from the other using a simple proportional rule.
    </p>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>The amount of fuel (<m>F</m>) used by a generator varies directly as the number of hours (<m>h</m>) it runs. If the generator consumes 4 liters of fuel in 2 hours, calculate the fuel used in 7 hours.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p><m>F \propto h</m></p>
            <p><m>\therefore F = kh</m></p>
            <p><em>Find the constant</em> <m>k</m>:</p>
            <p>Substituting <m>F = 4</m> when <m>h = 2</m></p>
            <p><m>4 = k \times 2</m></p>
            <p><m>k = \frac{4}{2} = 2</m></p>
            <p><em>Form the equation:</em></p>
            <p><m>F = 2h</m></p>
            <p><em>Calculate for</em> <m>h = 7</m>:</p>
            <p><m>F = 2 \times 7 = 14 \text{ liters}</m></p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>The extension (<m>e</m>) of a spring varies directly as the force (<m>F</m>) applied to it. A force of 10 Newtons causes an extension of 2 cm. Find the extension when a force of 25 Newtons is applied.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p><m>e \propto F</m></p>
            <p><m>\therefore e = kF</m></p>
            <p><em>Find the constant</em> <m>k</m>:</p>
            <p>Substituting <m>e = 2</m> when <m>F = 10</m></p>
            <p><m>2 = k \times 10</m></p>
            <p><m>k = \frac{2}{10} = \frac{1}{5} = 0.2</m></p>
            <p><em>Form the equation:</em></p>
            <p><m>e = 0.2F</m></p>
            <p><em>Calculate for</em> <m>F = 25</m>:</p>
            <p><m>e = 0.2 \times 25 = 5 \text{ cm}</m></p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>The total pay (<m>P</m>) received by a part-time worker is directly proportional to the number of days (<m>d</m>) worked. If the worker earns 3,600 sh. for 3 days of work, how much will they earn for 10 days?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p><m>P \propto d</m></p>
            <p><m>\therefore P = kd</m></p>
            <p><em>Find the constant</em> <m>k</m>:</p>
            <p>Substituting <m>P = 3600</m> when <m>d = 3</m></p>
            <p><m>3600 = k \times 3</m></p>
            <p><m>k = \frac{3600}{3} = 1200</m></p>
            <p>(This is the daily rate)</p>
            <p><em>Form the equation:</em></p>
            <p><m>P = 1200d</m></p>
            <p><em>Calculate for</em> <m>d = 10</m>:</p>
            <p><m>P = 1200 \times 10 = 12,000 \text{ sh.}</m></p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>The table below shows the amount of interest (<m>I</m>) earned on a savings account over a period of months (<m>m</m>).</p>
            <table>
                <title>Monthly Savings Interest</title>
                <tabular halign="left">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                        <cell>Months (<m>m</m>)</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                        <cell>6</cell>
                        <cell>8</cell>
                        <cell>10</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Interest (<m>I</m>) in sh.</cell>
                        <cell>50</cell>
                        <cell>100</cell>
                        <cell>150</cell>
                        <cell>200</cell>
                        <cell>250</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
    
                <item>
                    <p>(a) Draw the graph representing this information.</p>
                </item>
                <item>
                    <p>(b) Use the graph to find the constant of proportionality.</p>
                </item>
            
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>From the graph, we pick two points to find the gradient: <m>(4, 100)</m> and <m>(8, 200)</m>.</p>
            <p><m>\text{Gradient} = \frac{200 - 100}{8 - 4} = \frac{100}{4} = 25</m></p>
            <p><m>\text{Gradient} = \frac{I}{m} = 25</m></p>
            <p><m>I = 25m</m> is the equation relating <m>I</m> and <m>m</m>, and <em>25</em> is the constant of proportionality.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>The table below shows the weight (<m>W</m>) of a liquid in grams compared to its volume (<m>V</m>) in cubic centimeters.</p>
            <table>
                <title>Liquid Weight and Volume</title>
                <tabular halign="left">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                        <cell>Volume (<m>V</m>) in cm<m>^3</m></cell>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell>10</cell>
                        <cell>15</cell>
                        <cell>20</cell>
                        <cell>25</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Weight (<m>W</m>) in g</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                        <cell>8</cell>
                        <cell>12</cell>
                        <cell>16</cell>
                        <cell>20</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            
                <item>
                    <p>(a) Draw the graph representing this information.</p>
                </item>
                <item>
                    <p>(b) Use the graph to find the constant of proportionality.</p>
                </item>
            
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>From the graph, we pick two points to find the gradient: <m>(10, 8)</m> and <m>(20, 16)</m>.</p>
            <p><m>\text{Gradient} = \frac{16 - 8}{20 - 10} = \frac{8}{10} = 0.8</m></p>
            <p><m>\text{Gradient} = \frac{W}{V} = 0.8</m></p>
            <p><m>W = 0.8V</m> is the equation relating <m>W</m> and <m>V</m>, and <em>0.8</em> (the density of the liquid) is the constant of proportionality.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <!-- If STACK questions are included, uncomment this: -->

    <!-- Add STACK exercises if made -->

 <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The total pay (<m>P</m>) received by a casual worker depends on the number 
                of days (<m>d</m>) worked and the agreed daily wage rate (<m>w</m>) in shillings. 
                The relationship is given by:
            </p>
            <p>
                <me>P = wd</me>
            </p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li><p>Make the daily wage rate (<m>w</m>) the subject of the formula.</p></li>
                <li><p>Find <m>w</m> when <m>P = 3{,}600</m> and <m>d = 3</m>.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                (a) Dividing both sides by <m>d</m>:
                <me>w = \frac{P}{d}</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                (b) Substituting <m>P = 3600</m> and <m>d = 3</m>:
                <me>w = \frac{3600}{3} = 1200 \text{ sh. per day}</me>
            </p>
        </answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The extension (<m>e</m>) of a spring varies directly as the force (<m>F</m>) applied 
                to it. A scientist studying Hooke's Law records that a spring stretches by 
                2 cm when a force of 10 Newtons is applied. The relationship between extension 
                and force is given by:
            </p>
            <p>
                <me>e = kF</me>
            </p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li><p>Find the constant of proportionality (<m>k</m>).</p></li>
                <li><p>Find <m>e</m> when <m>F = 25</m> Newtons.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                (a) Substituting <m>e = 2</m> when <m>F = 10</m>:
                <me>2 = k \times 10 \Rightarrow k = \frac{2}{10} = 0.2</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                (b) Using the equation <m>e = 0.2F</m>:
                <me>e = 0.2 \times 25 = 5 \text{ cm}</me>
            </p>
        </answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A fuel generator at a construction site consumes diesel at a constant rate. 
                The total fuel consumed (<m>F</m>) in litres depends on the number of hours 
                (<m>h</m>) the generator runs. The generator is known to use 4 litres in 
                2 hours. The relationship is:
            </p>
            <p>
                <me>F = kh</me>
            </p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li><p>Find the constant of proportionality (<m>k</m>) and write the equation connecting <m>F</m> and <m>h</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Find <m>F</m> when the generator runs for <m>h = 7</m> hours.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                (a) Substituting <m>F = 4</m> when <m>h = 2</m>:
                <me>4 = k \times 2 \Rightarrow k = 2</me>
                The equation is <m>F = 2h</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                (b) Using <m>F = 2h</m>:
                <me>F = 2 \times 7 = 14 \text{ litres}</me>
            </p>
        </answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                In physics, the weight (<m>w</m>) of an object varies directly as the square 
                of its velocity (<m>z</m>) when calculating kinetic energy. In an experiment, 
                a moving object has a recorded value of <m>w = 18</m> when <m>z = 3</m>. 
                The relationship is:
            </p>
            <p>
                <me>w = kz^2</me>
            </p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li><p>Find the constant of proportionality (<m>k</m>).</p></li>
                <li><p>Find <m>w</m> when <m>z = 5</m>.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                (a) Substituting <m>w = 18</m> when <m>z = 3</m>:
                <me>18 = k(3^2) = 9k \Rightarrow k = 2</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                (b) Using <m>w = 2z^2</m>:
                <me>w = 2(5^2) = 2 \times 25 = 50</me>
            </p>
        </answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A trader selling maize flour charges a fixed price per kilogram. The total 
                cost (<m>p</m>) in shillings is directly proportional to the quantity purchased 
                (<m>q</m>) in kilograms. It is known that the difference in cost between 
                buying 7 kg and buying 3 kg is 20 shillings. The relationship is:
            </p>
            <p>
                <me>p = kq</me>
            </p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li><p>Find the constant of proportionality (<m>k</m>), which represents the price per kilogram.</p></li>
                <li><p>Find the cost of buying 10 kg of maize flour.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                (a) The cost at <m>q = 7</m> is <m>7k</m> and at <m>q = 3</m> is <m>3k</m>. 
                Their difference:
                <me>7k - 3k = 20 \Rightarrow 4k = 20 \Rightarrow k = 5 \text{ sh. per kg}</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                (b) Using <m>p = 5q</m>:
                <me>p = 5 \times 10 = 50 \text{ sh.}</me>
            </p>
        </answer>
    </exercise>
</exercises>

   <axiom component="lesson-plan">
    <title>Differentiation</title>

    <table>
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <tabular halign="left">
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                <cell>Strategy &amp; Learning Experience</cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                <cell>
                    <line>Provide partially completed tables and guided questions to help learners identify the constant ratio.</line>
                    <line>Pair learners with supportive peers during group activities.</line>
                    <line>Use concrete examples such as money, distance, and shadow measurements before introducing algebraic notation.</line>
                </cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                <cell>
                    <line>The core lesson activities as described above.</line>
                </cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                <cell>
                    <line>Challenge learners to derive direct variation equations independently from unfamiliar contexts.</line>
                    <line>Ask learners to determine the constant of proportionality from graphs and justify their reasoning.</line>
                    <line>Provide extension tasks involving direct variation with powers, such as quantities varying directly as the square of another quantity.</line>
                </cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
    </table>
</axiom>

